# Private key material never leaves this machine.
#
# The old approach was a global `*secret*` rule that excluded this whole
# directory. That is what caused the bootstrap paradox: the Syncthing config
# needed to join the fleet lived only inside $SYNC, which Syncthing had not set
# up yet. The key to the house was inside the house.
#
# The fix is to commit CIPHERTEXT and public recipients, and exclude only the
# private halves.

# Real age private keys -- never commit.
test-identity.txt
test-backup-identity.txt
*-private.txt
*.key

# Explicitly allowed (ciphertext + public material):
#   recipients.txt          public age recipients
#   kit-manifest.txt        list of paths, no secrets
#   bootstrap.tar.age       ciphertext, safe in a public repo
#   yubikey-identity.txt    NON-SECRET: names the PIV slot; the private key
#                           never leaves the hardware

# --- NOT yet safe to publish ---------------------------------------------
# Encrypted to a SINGLE scrypt (passphrase) recipient -- confirmed with
# age-inspect. A passphrase-only blob in a public repo is offline-crackable,
# and one recipient means no survivable key loss. Do not commit until it has
# been rebuilt as part of bootstrap.tar.age with the multi-recipient set
# (YubiKey A + YubiKey B + offline paper key).
syncthing-bootstrap.xml.age

# Syncthing device IDs. Not secret (they are exchanged with peers), but they
# identify your machines. Left uncommitted pending a deliberate decision.
known-devices.txt
